#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134701} :activity/id #uuid "c184b7bf-d80e-522a-9bde-709e111cc316" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049845 :topic/id #uuid "183bab17-91bb-560a-b6e6-758a6cc0dfcd" :topic/math-academy-id 2168} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134701 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135061 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the least-squares problem for the system\n\n$$A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}.$$\n\nWe can use QR factorization to construct the least-squares solution of this system. Recall that finding the QR factorization of $A$ means expressing $A$ in the form\n\n$$A = QR,$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $Q$ has orthonormal columns, and\n- $R$ is an upper triangular matrix with positive entries on its main diagonal.\n\nTo see how QR factorization can be used to solve a least-squares problem, we first recall that the least-squares solution satisfies the normal equation\n\n$$A^T A \\mathbf{x} = A^T \\mathbf{b}.$$\n\nSubstituting $A = QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, we have $Q^T Q = I.$ Therefore, the above equation becomes\n\n$$R^TR\\mathbf{x} = R^TQ^T\\mathbf{b}.$$\n\nSince $R^T$ is invertible, pre-multiplying our equation by $(R^T)^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b\\end{aligned}$$ which simplifies as\n\n$$R \\mathbf{x} = Q^T \\mathbf{b}. \\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)$$\n\nThe least-squares solution $\\hat{\\mathbf x}$ can now be found by solving this equation using back-substitution or row operations. This is usually the fastest way.\n\nIf we want an explicit formula for the least-squares solution, then we note that since $R$ is invertible, pre-multiplying $(\\ast)$ by $R^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{R}^{-1}\\cdot Rx & ={R}^{-1}\\cdot {Q}^{T}b\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\\mathbf{x} = R^{-1} Q^T \\mathbf{b}.$$\n\nThe least-squares solution is unique if $A$ has linearly independent columns." :tutorial/id #uuid "7a9501fc-fdbc-5905-ad31-e3c6c2767a33" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3639 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "733946c8-bb91-5523-9be6-6e334aa51df6" :step/math-academy-id 20276 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134887}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134702 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133803 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133612 :question/id #uuid "5fb2571c-2ab9-5fe5-9454-f9d7b0e89854" :question/math-academy-id "e-5592"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d177b305-cdd1-5f91-b9d5-90623e52ad10" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050267}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134334} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363161} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363168} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388625} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410810} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410811}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given a QR Factorization"} :step/id #uuid "eeb32ce1-92f7-5c9a-8d57-a6fb5f276cdf" :step/math-academy-id 20278} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134887 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133906 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133719 :question/id #uuid "d805b370-4377-5101-8ac7-3d30e11156ba" :question/math-academy-id "e-9917"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7d226d3-f7d3-574f-94f9-a532ec99b0ac" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050130}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134110} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401090} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410809} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433684} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440840}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Expression for a Least-Squares Solution in Terms of the Matrices Q and R"} :step/id #uuid "eab53e14-a261-5ebb-9ee3-6d39662a78cf" :step/math-academy-id 37297 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134889}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134889 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135143 :tutorial/content "Let's find the least-squares solution to $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b},$ where $A = QR,$ and $$Q = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}], \\qquad R = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}], \\qquad \\mathbf{b} = [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$$\n\nPreviously, we showed that the normal equation corresponding to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b},$ where $A=QR,$ can be reduced to\n\n$$R \\mathbf{x} = Q^T \\mathbf{b}.$$\n\nSubstituting our matrices into this equation, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$${\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}=6.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x_2=2.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4(2) & =2 \\\\ 2{x}_{1} & =10 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =5.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}$ is $[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5fcac262-421e-57b7-b038-713427320ff4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6150 :tutorial/title "Using QR Factorization to Solve a Least-Squares Problem"} :step/id #uuid "f3959285-3101-555f-96a2-bd9e95bfe12e" :step/math-academy-id 37330 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134702}}] :activity/title "Finding a Least-Squares Solution Using QR Factorization" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134028}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410813} :activity/id #uuid "9b29a2c1-0e8f-51a5-8bb4-88c6d8d5a680" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049845 :topic/id #uuid "183bab17-91bb-560a-b6e6-758a6cc0dfcd" :topic/math-academy-id 2168} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410812 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410809 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39d0e813-26d6-5597-ad13-db18f30203df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${A}^{T}$ and $b$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410786} {:answer/id #uuid "4f27a535-7b11-525f-9c56-8b5ab7bcd664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${R}^{-1}$ and $b$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410787} {:answer/id #uuid "9cef819e-2e48-567b-8a8f-3202e6986c5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${b}^{T}$ and $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410793} {:answer/id #uuid "e0801752-ba2f-56ca-be80-5bf37502d69c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${b}^{T}$ and $R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410800} {:answer/id #uuid "e6e67057-98e7-5f3b-a5f1-858830ac094c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$R$ and $b$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410801}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410787} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8574fe5-c6b2-5e0e-b6b5-71b14a1ca898" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410807}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6c899a77-435a-5aa3-92d4-0dc3a7b627aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-158080" :question/problem "$$\nx=\\,{Q}^{T}\\,\n$$\n\nLet $A=QR$  be a QR decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, and the expression above gives the least-squares solution of $Ax=b.$ From left to right, what are the missing parts of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the normal equation\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nThen, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $R$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by ${R}^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{-1}\\cdot Rx & ={R}^{-1}\\cdot {Q}^{T}b \\\\ x & ={R}^{-1}\\,\\,{Q}^{T}\\,\\,b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing parts of the equation are ${R}^{-1}$ and $b.$"} :step/id #uuid "6b34aecb-fc33-591a-beae-813382c608f3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410815}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410813 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134112 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05f55e0b-2bfd-5324-bb60-ef44a8ce8695" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$R$ and ${b}^{T}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410778} {:answer/id #uuid "0acc060c-c80f-5318-9ceb-31102a3b5205" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${R}^{-1}$ and $b$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410780} {:answer/id #uuid "accbb9fd-1e2b-5eb4-9d55-1c270308aa87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${R}^{-1}$ and $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410796} {:answer/id #uuid "d56e2344-c38a-5d60-89d9-ddd844e5f790" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$R$ and $b$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410798} {:answer/id #uuid "e8d265c7-1332-5802-b568-b47af3ef47f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${R}^{T}$ and $b$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410802}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410780} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d57014e-b23a-5701-a76a-a3703de7149c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410805}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "285a0657-649f-57c4-8ca1-f228238070d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-139773" :question/problem "$$\nx=\\,{Q}^{T}\\,\n$$\n\nLet $A=QR$  be a QR decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, and the expression above gives the least-squares solution of $Ax=b.$ From left to right, what are the missing parts of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the normal equation\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nThen, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $R$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by ${R}^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{-1}\\cdot Rx & ={R}^{-1}\\cdot {Q}^{T}b \\\\ x & ={R}^{-1}\\,\\,{Q}^{T}\\,\\,b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing parts of the equation are ${R}^{-1}$ and $b.$"} :step/id #uuid "70103913-067c-52aa-850c-673f2ce5ac0f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410816}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410814 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410810 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12a0bfb2-9ec4-5669-b98c-557ad2a9bc4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410782} {:answer/id #uuid "2af5f523-a673-51cf-8e9d-4a4edaf229e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410785} {:answer/id #uuid "6bc6960e-7f53-5f78-93da-de1e72ff8ab4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410789} {:answer/id #uuid "a27e8c2d-50df-58c3-85c9-4e38ce8d2589" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410794} {:answer/id #uuid "be9612fa-59cc-5f2d-8433-f16960b6ff97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410797}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410794} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a8d53f7-b5fc-5ce6-9a9c-54a7385b4356" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410806}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6e2326cd-c939-5766-9e1e-50b935159886" :question/math-academy-id "q-157095" :question/problem "Given the QR decomposition $A=QR,$ find the least-squares solution to $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nQ=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14} & 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{14} & -3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{14} & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,R=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14} & 3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14} & 3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14} & \\sqrt{14} & \\sqrt{14} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3} & -3\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14} & 3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}-3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14}{x}_{1}+3\\sqrt{14}{x}_{2}=-3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{3}{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14}{x}_{1}+3\\sqrt{14}(0) & =-3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ \\sqrt{14}{x}_{1} & =-3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$"} :step/id #uuid "8ef06eb7-a320-54c5-a67e-7c28d93f3d07" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410812}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410815 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410808 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09435933-4c28-52dd-8678-41dcf0848333" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}^{T}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410779} {:answer/id #uuid "24985731-b6b9-5e8d-820b-1f13827d0949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(R-m\\cdot I)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410783} {:answer/id #uuid "50d2e581-451b-5360-b4da-e26396ea468e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(R-I)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410788} {:answer/id #uuid "7dceedd7-4b1f-5a60-b090-4dc26f3702e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410790} {:answer/id #uuid "91cae48a-5ff2-5a13-a3b1-20c945ab4731" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}^{T}R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410792}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410790} :answer-field/id #uuid "3eb41613-58b3-5878-8f3f-4404da6faf4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4e50c9b4-d5fe-527c-b554-c5ae45ba5355" :question/math-academy-id "q-158081" :question/problem "$$\nx=\\,{Q}^{T}\\,b\n$$\n\nLet $A=QR$  be a QR decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, and the expression above gives the least-squares solution of $Ax=b.$ What is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the normal equation\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nThen, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $R$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by ${R}^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{-1}\\cdot Rx & ={R}^{-1}\\cdot {Q}^{T}b \\\\ x & ={R}^{-1}\\,\\,{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing part of the equation is ${R}^{-1}.$"} :step/id #uuid "d783bc27-1d4d-5fd3-a3db-1a036831d9dd"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410816 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410811 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cfa9745-27c9-5c69-9259-9f33cdd30cbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410781} {:answer/id #uuid "290a80b2-d833-5e18-ba11-922da75fadbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410784} {:answer/id #uuid "84becdeb-7cb6-51f9-8247-37058f839cfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410791} {:answer/id #uuid "ab331c8e-8ec8-5a70-b8c2-e3efcd32bf48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410795} {:answer/id #uuid "d9a2f5a3-da1b-5892-8fa1-25b99c4d869f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410799}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410795} :answer-field/id #uuid "44672c62-c008-5400-a940-292eccf518a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c8892a71-2970-5d65-891c-76f1124825ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-139796" :question/problem "Given the QR decomposition $A=QR,$ find the least-squares solution to $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nQ=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2} & -1 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} & -1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,R=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2} & -4\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2} & -4\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2} & 2\\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -1 & -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2} & -4\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}38\\sqrt{2} \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}-4\\sqrt{2}{x}_{2}=38\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-5.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}-4\\sqrt{2}(-5) & =38\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1} & =18\\sqrt{2} \\\\ {x}_{1} & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].$"} :step/id #uuid "de997394-83f9-5ca3-92c7-b205bedc19d1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410814}}] :activity/title "Finding a Least-Squares Solution Using QR Factorization" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410777}]}
